            "title": "cat << 'EOF' > conductor/plan.md # Implementation Plan: Psychiatric Readmission Analysis Updates ## Objective Update the `Readmission_Full_Analysis.ipynb` notebook to improve CatBoost configuration, implement 5-fold stratified cross-validation across all models, generalize the clinical burden vs. benefit visualizations, and augment specific demographic/clinical plots with statistical significance indicators and confidence intervals. Adjust modeling features based on new C-SSRS definitions, interactions, and standardizations. ## Key Files & Context - `Readmission_Full_Analysis.ipynb`: The primary notebook where all modeling and visualization changes will be implemented. ## Implementation Steps ### 1. Model Configuration & Cross-Validation - **5-Fold Stratified CV**: Locate the cross-validation definition (`cv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=10)`) and update to `n_splits=5`. - **CatBoost One-Hot Encoding**: - Dynamically define `cat_features_idx` before model initialization: identify all column indices in `X_ml` that have 10 or fewer unique values. - Cast these specific categorical columns to integers to satisfy CatBoost's type requirements. - Ensure the `CatBoostClassifier` initialization correctly accepts `cat_features_idx` alongside the existing `one_hot_max_size=10` parameter. ### 2. Feature Engineering & Logistic Regression Updates - **Standardize Continuous Variables**: Standard-scale (z-score) continuous variables like `los_days`, `age`, and `complexity_x_age` before fitting the Logistic Regression model. This will ensure their Odds Ratios represent a 1-standard-deviation increase, making their Confidence Intervals visible and interpretable in the forest plot (Fig 7). - **C-SSRS Level**: Replace `csrss_any` with an ordinal `csrss_level` variable (0 = No flag, 1 = Yellow, 2 = Orange, 3 = Red). - **Interaction Terms**: - Update `nssi_x_csrss` to specifically represent `has_nssi * csrss_red` (NSSI $\\times$ Red Flag), instead of ANY flag. - Add a new interaction term: `age_x_public_insurance` (`age * insurance_public`). - **Category Renaming**: Rename the \"Other Mental Disorder\" DSM-5 category to \"Homicidal Ideation\" across the analysis, as underlying data reveals it is composed 100% of the ICD-10 code R45.850. ### 3. FAMD & UMAP Visualization Enhancements - **Descriptive Axes**: Locate the UMAP visualization code (Fig 12) and update the generic labels (`UMAP 1`, `UMAP 2`) to descriptive names representing the embedded variance (e.g., `UMAP Dimension 1 (Clinical/Demographic Profile)` and `UMAP Dimension 2 (Diagnostic Complexity)`). ### 4. Clinical Burden vs. Benefit and FP/FN Graphs - **Dynamic Probability Thresholding**: Calculate the exact threshold where False Positives (FP) equal False Negatives (FN) (the crossover point). Update the `key_thresholds` array for the Clinical Burden vs. Benefit graph (Fig 14) so that the maximum threshold displayed is this crossover point. - **Multi-Model Visualizations**: Refactor the plotting logic for Fig 13 (FP vs FN) and Fig 14 (Burden vs Benefit) into a loop or reusable function. Execute this logic for the predicted probabilities of all three models: CatBoost, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression. Save the additional figures with appropriate suffixes. ### 5. Confidence Intervals & Statistical Indicators (p < 0.05) - **Fig 1 (Age Distribution)**: - Increase the vertical axis limit so the confidence interval for age 6 is fully visible. - Perform a statistical test comparing the readmission rate of each age against the rest of the cohort. Annotate bars with a `*` indicator if $p < 0.05$. - **Fig 2 (Demographics)**: - Update headers: \"Gender ID at Admission\", and \"Insurance Type\". - Add standard error/confidence interval bars to all binary charts. - Calculate p-values for each binary split and append statistical significance to the titles or above the bars. - **Fig 3 (DSM-5 Categories)**: - Add 95% Confidence Intervals for each category's readmission rate. - Color a category's bar ORANGE *only if* its $n \\ge 15$ AND the lower bound of its CI is strictly greater than the overall readmission rate (approx. 9.3%). Otherwise, color it BLUE. - **Fig 4 (Diagnostic Complexity)**: - Calculate exact numerical boundaries for the `qcut` quartiles (e.g., \"Q1 (1-2)\", \"Q2 (3)\", etc.) and use them as x-axis labels. - Make all bars in the right-side plot the same color. - Calculate p-values comparing the readmission rate in each complexity quartile against the baseline (Q1) and add significance markers (`*`). - **Fig 5 (C-SSRS and ACE Score)**: - Include a specific comparison (with CI and p-value) of the readmission rate for \"No C-SSRS Flag\" versus \"Red Flag\". - Replace or supplement the continuous ACE score boxplot with a binary bar chart comparing \"ACE score $\\ge 4$\" vs \"ACE score $\\le 3$\", including confidence intervals and a p-value. ## Verification & Testing - Use `nbformat` to safely apply changes and run `jupyter nbconvert --execute` to ensure all cells execute without errors. - Verify `cat_features_idx` passes into CatBoost without type errors. - Inspect the generated images in `figures/` to confirm all formatting, labels, significance markers, and model-specific graphs render correctly. EOF",
