    {
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "All three cells are added. Here's what the analysis will tell you, plus your post-hoc options:\n\n---\n\n**What the UMAP correlation cells produce:**\n\n- **Fig 12b** \u2014 Ranked bar charts of Spearman r for every feature vs. UMAP 1 and UMAP 2. Once you run it you'll see which clinical variables actually structure the embedding (e.g., if `n_dsm5_categories`, `los_days`, and `has_medical_comorbidity` dominate UMAP 1, the axis represents *multi-system complexity*; if `age` and `has_asd` dominate UMAP 2, it's an *age/neurodevelopmental* axis). The printed table also shows significance stars.\n- **Fig 12c** \u2014 The actual UMAP cloud recolored by each top feature, so you can visually confirm whether structure tracks the correlations.\n\n---\n\n**Recommended post-hoc analyses (in rough priority order for a clinical paper):**\n\n**1. Decision Curve Analysis (DCA)**  \nArguably the most clinically meaningful analysis. DCA plots *net benefit* at every decision threshold, letting you compare CatBoost vs. treat-all vs. treat-none under different assumptions about the FP/FN cost ratio. It directly answers \"at what threshold is screening with this model actually worth it?\" The `dcurves` pip package implements it cleanly.\n\n**2. Model calibration (reliability diagrams)**  \nYour probabilities need to be well-calibrated for clinical use \u2014 if CatBoost outputs p=0.25, does 25% of those patients actually readmit? `sklearn.calibration.calibration_curve` + `CalibrationDisplay` does this in a few lines. You'd run it for all three models. Miscalibration can be corrected with Platt scaling or isotonic regression.\n\n**3. Subgroup performance (equity analysis)**  \nCompute AUC, sensitivity, and PPV broken down by: age group (\u22649 vs. 10\u201312), sex, insurance type, ACE \u22654 vs. <4, and ACE missing vs. present. This is increasingly required for clinical AI papers and will reveal whether the model degrades for any subgroup.\n\n**4. UMAP cluster profiling with HDBSCAN**  \nRun HDBSCAN on the UMAP coordinates to find natural patient clusters, then produce a Table 1-style comparison across clusters (rates, diagnoses, demographics). This turns the UMAP from a pretty picture into a clinically interpretable patient typology.\n\n**5. SHAP interaction values**  \n`shap.TreeExplainer(cat_full).shap_interaction_values(X_ml)` returns an n\u00d7p\u00d7p matrix. You can plot the top interaction pairs as a heatmap \u2014 this reveals, e.g., whether the effect of medical comorbidity on risk depends on age or ACE score.\n\n**6. FAMD loadings heatmap**  \n`famd.column_correlations_` gives the correlation of each feature with each FAMD component. A heatmap of the top components \u00d7 features will make the UMAP interpretation more rigorous and is methodologically more correct than the UMAP Spearman approach (since UMAP 1 is shaped by FAMD, not directly by the raw features).\n\n**7. DeLong test for AUC comparison**  \nTests whether the difference in AUC between CatBoost and RF is statistically significant. The `scipy` and `rpy2` stacks both have implementations; there's also a pure-Python `delong` package.",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1780286650.4299278,
      "id": "6ccefa38-1916-453d-9976-37517376d583",
