    {
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "ffd55ec2-a5b4-4edc-9694-5ef2c808511c",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1780302838.4985092,
      "body": "Done. When you re-run from Cell 1, you'll see output like:\n\n```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n  \u26a0  DATE QUALITY FLAGS \u2014 N records excluded\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n  Missing VisitDate   : 0\n  Missing DischDate   : 0\n  Negative LOS        : N  (range: -2 to -1 days)\n\n  Readmission status of flagged records:\n    Readmitted     : X\n    Not readmitted : Y\n\n  Saved for review \u2192 flagged_records_date_errors.csv\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```\n\nThe flagged records are written to `flagged_records_date_errors.csv` with a `flag_reason` column so you can inspect the raw dates and decide whether they're correctable (typo in the year, day/month swap, etc.) or should be permanently excluded. Until you confirm, they're out of `df` and therefore out of every model, figure, and table downstream. Re-running the full notebook from Cell 1 will give you clean N counts everywhere."
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1780303027.6304471,
      "mentions": [
        "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      ],
      "body": "@Claude  Starting in the SHAP dependence graph for catboost it says 1679 records for LOS have negative values--this seems implausible. That's more than 50% of the records. ",
      "sender": "66b5de9aa1ae4e47a45824fbb597caf1",
      "id": "0c651e51-38ee-4eb6-a9c1-5eb42fe58e31"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1780303204.705499,
      "id": "11ba1a52-1ad3-434d-9eaa-b31b7684b0e6",
      "body": "The 1679 count is almost certainly a kernel state issue, not a genuine data problem. Here's what happened:\n\n**The sequence:** You ran Cell 1 with the new date filter (which drops negative LOS records from `df`), but `X_ml` in memory was computed from a **previous run of Cell 14** \u2014 before the filter existed. That stale `X_ml` still contains the full original dataset, so the warning I added in the SHAP cell is seeing the old unfiltered data. The \"1679\" is not 1,679 records with erroneous dates \u2014 it's the gap between what `df` is now and what `X_ml` still is.\n\nThis is a classic notebook consistency problem. You need a full clean run:\n\n**\u2192 Kernel \u2192 Restart & Run All**\n\nAfter that, Cell 1 will print the actual count of flagged records (likely a small number \u2014 single digits to low tens). Cell 14 will rebuild `X_ml` from the filtered `df`. The SHAP warning will then reflect the clean data and should show 0 or a plausible number.\n\nIf Cell 1 still reports something implausibly large after a full restart (e.g., hundreds of records), that would mean the dates in the CSV have a systematic format issue \u2014 for example, DD/MM/YYYY being parsed as MM/DD/YYYY \u2014 which would cause large-scale sign flips in LOS. In that case, come back and we'll investigate the raw date strings directly. But I'd expect the restart to resolve it.",
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
      "time": 1780303312.572842,
      "mentions": [
        "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::GeminiAcpPersona"
      ],
      "body": "@Gemini I tried to restart the kernel and the server and I get this issue: \"---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImportError                               Traceback (most recent call last)\nCell In[1], line 8\n      4 import matplotlib.patches as mpatches\n      5 import matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\n      6 import seaborn as sns\n      7 from scipy import stats\n----> 8 import statsmodels.api as sm\n      9 import statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n     10 from sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold, cross_val_predict\n     11 from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nFile ~/code/jupyter_projects/.venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/statsmodels/api.py:76\n      1 __all__ = [\n      2     \"BayesGaussMI\",\n      3     \"BinomialBayesMixedGLM\",\n   (...)     72     \"__version_info__\"\n     73 ]\n---> 76 from . import datasets, distributions, iolib, regression, robust, tools\n     77 from .__init__ import test\n     78 from statsmodels._version import (\n     79     version as __version__, version_tuple as __version_info__\n     80 )\n\nFile ~/code/jupyter_projects/.venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/statsmodels/distributions/__init__.py:7\n      2 from .empirical_distribution import (\n      3     ECDF, ECDFDiscrete, monotone_fn_inverter, StepFunction\n      4     )\n      5 from .edgeworth import ExpandedNormal\n----> 7 from .discrete import (\n      8     genpoisson_p, zipoisson, zigenpoisson, zinegbin,\n      9     )\n     11 __all__ = [\n     12     'ECDF',\n     13     'ECDFDiscrete',\n   (...)     21     'zipoisson'\n     22     ]\n     24 test = PytestTester()\n\nFile ~/code/jupyter_projects/.venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/statsmodels/distributions/discrete.py:5\n      3 from scipy.stats import rv_discrete, poisson, nbinom\n      4 from scipy.special import gammaln\n----> 5 from scipy._lib._util import _lazywhere\n      7 from statsmodels.base.model import GenericLikelihoodModel\n     10 class genpoisson_p_gen(rv_discrete):\n\nImportError: cannot import name '_lazywhere' from 'scipy._lib._util' (/Users/matthewgold/code/jupyter_projects/.venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/scipy/_lib/_util.py)\"",
